<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Sonny Liston is a 37-year-old professional heavyweight boxer with stage 2 hypertension and moderate alcohol use disorder who comes for his yearly preventive visit, three weeks after his four-week review. He takes amlodipine 10 mg and valsartan 160 mg each morning and naltrexone 50 mg each morning, and shows the picture card he keeps in his gym bag. His home readings, which the talking monitor reads out and he leaves on the clinic voice line, average 128/80 mmHg. Since starting naltrexone he has three or four drinks on two evenings a week and none on other days. Mild ankle swelling after long runs in the heat goes by morning. He has had no chest pain, breathlessness or palpitations. He wants to be cleared to fight again this fall. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Visit</td><td>Periodic adult preventive visit, age 37</td></tr><tr><td>Blood pressure</td><td>Clinic 128/80 mmHg; home average 128/80 mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>Alcohol</td><td>Three or four drinks on two evenings a week on naltrexone</td></tr><tr><td>Mood</td><td>PHQ-2 0</td></tr><tr><td>Medicines</td><td>Amlodipine 10 mg and valsartan 160 mg; naltrexone 50 mg</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Health risk assessment. Asked aloud by the nurse in a private room, one question at a time, with answers recorded for him; reviewed by Dr. Achebe-Whitford. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Finding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-rated health</td><td>Good; feels stronger since drinking less</td></tr><tr><td>Psychosocial</td><td>Married; close to his trainer and gym; money is steady from fighting and exhibitions</td></tr><tr><td>Tobacco</td><td>Does not smoke</td></tr><tr><td>Alcohol and drugs</td><td>Drinking reduced on naltrexone; no drug use (DAST-10 0 at intake)</td></tr><tr><td>Physical activity</td><td>Trains six days a week: roadwork, bag work and sparring</td></tr><tr><td>Nutrition</td><td>Large meals; eats out often; cutting down on salted meats and fast food</td></tr><tr><td>Sleep</td><td>Seven to eight hours</td></tr><tr><td>Driving and injury</td><td>Drives himself; wears a seat belt; agreed never to drive after drinking</td></tr><tr><td>Home safety</td><td>No firearms in the home; smoke detector working</td></tr><tr><td>Health literacy</td><td>Prefers spoken explanations and pictures; uses the picture card and leaves his readings on the clinic voice line</td></tr><tr><td>Dental</td><td>No dentist for many years; one back tooth sore when chewing</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

